## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of event-driven AWS architectures, video processing workflows, and end-to-end data indexing, with emphasis on security, reliability, and cost optimization.

## Key Concepts

- Event-driven orchestration using S3 event notifications and Step Functions
- AWS Elemental MediaConvert for multi-format video processing (MP4, GIF thumbnails)
- DynamoDB indexing with comprehensive video metadata
- Security best practices: IAM least privilege, encryption at rest and in transit, access logging
- Robust error handling: retry mechanisms, dead-letter queues, comprehensive monitoring
- Cost optimization strategies: appropriate resource sizing, storage lifecycle policies, and efficient processing patterns

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example Lambda snippet: update DynamoDB after MediaConvert completion
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const ddb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const { jobId, status, output } = event.detail;

  if (status === 'COMPLETE') {
    const params = {
      TableName: process.env.VIDEOS_TABLE,
      Item: {
        videoId: output.videoId,
        sourceKey: output.inputFile,
        thumbnailKey: output.thumbnailPath,
        previewKey: output.mp4Path,
        duration: output.duration,
        encodingQuality: '1080p',
        processedAt: new Date().toISOString()
      }
    };

    await ddb.put(params).promise();
  }
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end integration of AWS AI services with a clean event-driven pattern, including common NLU tasks (sentiment, themes) and a custom-entities layer for domain knowledge. It also probes data handling, privacy, and operational trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven architecture with S3 -> Lambda
- Amazon Comprehend: sentiment, key phrase/theme extraction
- Comprehend Custom Entities for product-domain data
- DynamoDB upsert semantics and partitioning strategy
- Data privacy: encryption (KMS), access logs, least privilege
- Reliability: idempotency, retries, error handling
- Cost: batching, thresholds, per-request pricing

## Code Example
```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const s3 = new AWS.S3();
const comprehend = new AWS.Comprehend();
const dynamo = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const rec = event.Records[0];
  const bucket = rec.s3.bucket.name;
  const key = decodeURIComponent(rec.s3.object.key.replace(/\+/g, ' '));
  const obj = await s3.getObject({Bucket: bucket, Key: key}).promise();
  const data = JSON.parse(obj.Body.toString('utf-8'));
  const text = data.text;
  const lang = data.language || 'en';

  const sentiment = (await comprehend.detectSentiment({Text: text, LanguageCode: lang}).promise()).Sentiment;
  const themes = (await comprehend.detectEntities({Text: text, LanguageCode: lang}).promise()).Entities.map(e => e.Type);

  await dynamo.put({
    TableName: 'FeedbackIndex',
    Item: {
      feedbackId: data.feedbackId,
      customerId: data.customerId,
      language: lang,
      sentiment,
      themes
    }
  }).promise();

  // Optionally write summary/output to S3 here
  return {status: 'ok'};
};
```

